#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101573} :activity/id #uuid "89da6fec-55ed-53dd-b5e1-c3ef975373c3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677 :topic/id #uuid "b3fe0bdc-d6d3-510d-ba60-be31a59b4ba0" :topic/math-academy-id 1157} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101977 :tutorial/content "One interesting number pattern is **Pascal's triangle**, named after Blaise Pascal, a famous French mathematician.\n\nPascal's triangle is made by rows of numbers that form a triangle.\nEach row has one more number than the row above.\nThe first two rows are made of three $1$s in a triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's construct the next row.\nThe first and last number in each row is always $1.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & ◻ &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe other numbers are always greater than $1$ and can be found by adding the two closest numbers located in the previous row.\n\nIn our case, the numbers closest to the unknown number and located in the previous row are ${\\color{blue}1}$ and ${\\color{blue}1}.$\nTherefore, the unknown number is the sum of $\\color{blue}1$ and ${\\color{blue}1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n{\\large\\square} = {\\color{blue}1} + {\\color{blue}1} = 2\n$$\n\nNow, we have the first three rows of Pascal's triangle.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we continue repeating this process for each row, we will get the following results for the first six rows of Pascal's triangle:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "f708f697-814a-58cd-8926-d278c8e15fc4" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3392 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9bab1b86-237d-59f2-b5b8-117d4edfdf46" :step/math-academy-id 105243 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100805 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100715 :question/id #uuid "4014e57c-3c50-5358-a9ae-280610aa6cfb" :question/math-academy-id "e-5107"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0392894c-6bbc-544a-8ab5-c0d2be332967" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101525} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101526} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492069}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Missing Number in a Pascal's Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "fdd95bd0-6aef-5462-959d-c840b6c96482" :step/math-academy-id 105244 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101975 :tutorial/content "Multiplying a binomial by itself $n$ times is known as a **binomial expansion**.\nFor instance, multiplying the binomial $(a + b)$ by itself gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2.\n$$\n\nLet's consider the binomial expansion of \n\n$$\n(a+b)^n,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a positive integer.\n\nTo write out the expansion, we use two patterns to simplify most of the hard work: the **exponent pattern** and the **coefficient pattern**.\n\n- The **exponent pattern** is\n \n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}n}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}{n-1}}b^{{\\color{blue}1}}, \\quad   a^{{\\color{red}n-2}}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  \\dots\\quad   a^{\\color{red}2}b^{{\\color{blue}n-2}},\\quad    a^{\\color{red}1}b^{{\\color{blue}n - 1}}, \\quad   a^{\\color{red}0}b^{{\\color{blue}n}}.\n$$\n\nThe exponents of $a$ start at $n$ and go down ($n, n-1, \\cdots, 1, 0$) while the exponents of $b$ increase from $0$ to $n.$ In every term, the two exponents always add up to $n.$\n\n- The **coefficient pattern** can be found using Pascal's Triangle:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} &  &  &  &  &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  &  & 1 &  & 2 &  & 1 \\\\  &  &  & 1 &  & 3 &  & 3 &  & 1 \\\\  &  & 1 &  & 4 &  & 6 &  & 4 &  & 1 \\\\  & 1 &  & 5 &  & 10 &  & 10 &  & 5 &  & 1 \\\\ ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮ &  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe take the top row to be the $0$th row. Then, the $n$th row of Pascal's Triangle are the coefficients of $(a+b)^n.$\n\nLet's use this to expand the binomial \n\n$$\n(a+b)^3.\n$$\n\nThe exponent pattern is\n\n$$\na^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0},\\quad  a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1}, \\quad a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2}, \\quad  a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nSince $n=3,$ the coefficient pattern is found using the $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle: $(1,3,3,1).$ \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3  \n= \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}3}b^{\\color{blue}0} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}2}b^{\\color{blue}1} + \\mathbf{3}a^{\\color{red}1}b^{\\color{blue}2} + \\mathbf{1}a^{\\color{red}0}b^{\\color{blue}3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, simplifying the above gives\n\n$$\n(a + b)^3= a^{3} + 3a^{2}b + 3ab^{2} + b^{3} ,\n$$\n\nand we're done!" :tutorial/id #uuid "5a75ca66-5c0a-578d-ad4a-170fe5297413" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3385 :tutorial/title "Expanding Binomials Using Pascal’s Triangle"} :step/id #uuid "cfeca023-2ab0-5fb8-acc2-eb8b7cb7c2b7" :step/math-academy-id 105247 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101576}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101576 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101990 :tutorial/content "The following table shows the first six binomial expansions.\n\n| **Power** | **Pascal's Triangle** | **Binomial** | **Binomial Expansion** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^0$ | $\\mathbf{1}$ |\n| $1$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^1$ | $\\mathbf{1}a + \\mathbf{1}b$ |\n| $2$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{2}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^2$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^2 + \\mathbf{2}ab + \\mathbf{1}b^2$ |\n| $3$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{3}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^3$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^3 + \\mathbf{3}a^2b + \\mathbf{3}ab^2 + \\mathbf{1}b^3$ |\n| $4$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{6}\\quad\\mathbf{4}\\quad\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^4$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^4 + \\mathbf{4}a^3b + \\mathbf{6}a^2b^2 + \\mathbf{4}ab^3 + \\mathbf{1}b^4$ |\n| $5$ | $\\mathbf{1}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\!\\quad\\!\\mathbf{10}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{5}\\quad\\!\\mathbf{1}$ | $(a + b)^5$ | $\\mathbf{1}a^5 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}a^4b \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^3b^2 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{10}a^2b^3 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{5}ab^4 \\!+\\! \\mathbf{1}b^5$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "249c4559-4588-59e9-ae28-c70bc71b66d6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3895 :tutorial/title "Table of Results"} :step/id #uuid "ad9aecb2-af07-5420-8809-96b51b344aea" :step/math-academy-id 105248 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101577}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101577 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100806 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100716 :question/id #uuid "57c2e733-51b4-51f5-8774-ebbd6da2321d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5152"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f386de30-38b8-59b7-a6e8-ea5e386671d5" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050802}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101400} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492054} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503745}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "9cb6d0cd-cd5c-55dc-b0de-0c0f8e98c8f9" :step/math-academy-id 105249 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101578}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101578 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100807 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100719 :question/id #uuid "bc866f37-17b6-59e0-9088-fd71a46c5b9a" :question/math-academy-id "e-5158"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dcb621ad-bcc5-57ea-9274-d2bbe93df2ce" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101117} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492006} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492045} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503741}] :knowledge-point/title "Expanding a Binomial With More Than One Variable"} :step/id #uuid "c9695aa6-0e5e-5dde-9090-7b976e64abda" :step/math-academy-id 105252 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101579}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101579 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100808 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186100720 :question/id #uuid "b3546d6e-8399-5694-85db-c73f0a57b95c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5159"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a99f4eb-5f34-565e-9375-7a35ec93a8c6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101116} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186491994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492029} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492051}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coefficient of a Particular Term in a Binomial Expansion"} :step/id #uuid "0b37dfbf-fe94-576b-a926-3f916a97dffa" :step/math-academy-id 105255}] :activity/title "Expanding Binomials Using Pascal's Triangle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186101026}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046648} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503805} :activity/id #uuid "9b7f87c5-a873-5d35-a338-1eb6f31f95ee" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048677 :topic/id #uuid "b3fe0bdc-d6d3-510d-ba60-be31a59b4ba0" :topic/math-academy-id 1157} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503765 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503727 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e0b8e5d-ea62-586b-acf8-47c7448fb9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "328" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503038} {:answer/id #uuid "3ad5cbb7-83ea-5127-9a88-0dafeb6f2337" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "136" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503091} {:answer/id #uuid "6adb48dc-a33c-5da7-ae77-576efafa9e3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "168" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503190} {:answer/id #uuid "83db7d2e-5441-54f0-9b72-b23bdd133079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "320" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503250} {:answer/id #uuid "eddbbe69-969c-5b54-965b-bba69c119ec3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "128" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503469}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503091} :answer-field/id #uuid "056e7899-7359-5aa3-9d75-0e24b35490d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503516}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab532852-8985-56d4-9487-9412ea181516" :question/math-academy-id "q-3564" :question/problem "Given that \n\n$$\n{(2{t}^{2}-3s)}^{4}=A{t}^{8}+B{t}^{6}s+C{t}^{4}{s}^{2}-216{t}^{2}{s}^{3}+81{s}^{4}\n$$\n\nwhere $A,B$ and $C$ are constants, determine the value of $A+B+C.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=4,$ $a=2{t}^{2},$ and $b=-3s.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{4}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{4}.\n$$\n\nThe $4$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,4,6,4,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2{t}^{2}-3s)^{4} & =1{a}^{4}+4{a}^{3}b+6{a}^{2}{b}^{2}+4a{b}^{3}+1{b}^{4} \\\\  & =1{(2{t}^{2})}^{4}(-3s)^{0}+4{(2{t}^{2})}^{3}(-3s)^{1}+6{(2{t}^{2})}^{2}(-3s)^{2} \\\\  & \\,+4{(2{t}^{2})}^{1}(-3s)^{3}+1{(2{t}^{2})}^{0}(-3s)^{4} \\\\  & =1(16{t}^{8})(1)+4(8{t}^{6})(-3s)+6(4{t}^{4})(9{s}^{2}) \\\\  & \\,+4(2{t}^{2})(-27{s}^{3})+1(1)(81{s}^{4}) \\\\  & =16{t}^{8}-96{t}^{6}s+216{t}^{4}{s}^{2}-216{t}^{2}{s}^{3}+81{s}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=16,B=-96,$ and $C=216.$ Finally,\n\n$$\nA+B+C=16-96+216=136.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "0fcd4859-f70e-5b53-af4b-3bf056bd41ce" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503841}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503745 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b600bcd-5006-5baf-9dca-4aab5033031d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+125{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503008} {:answer/id #uuid "4c839c43-5c26-510d-85ac-e2e3c39a216c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{4}+145{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503128} {:answer/id #uuid "4fb998d5-d716-53e2-a50b-72e1271eb277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}-100{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503133} {:answer/id #uuid "5135b701-cd58-5dde-8d9b-989995318cb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{4}+100{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503136} {:answer/id #uuid "9799692b-0119-5014-ae4f-3ef5e9145c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+145{y}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503281}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503008} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef1b5443-9d42-51aa-ac69-73ecc6958d74" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "d6a9fa51-d96f-5308-9675-af7e9df7f105" :question/math-academy-id "q-3563" :question/problem "Find, in ascending powers of $y,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=5,$ $a=10y,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{5}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{4}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{3}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{3},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{4},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{5}.\n$$\n\nThe $5$th row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,5,10,10,5,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(10y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} & =1{a}^{5}+5{a}^{4}b+10{a}^{3}{b}^{2}+10{a}^{2}{b}^{3}+5a{b}^{4}+1{b}^{5} \\\\  & =1(10y)^{5}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{0}+5(10y)^{4}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1}+10(10y)^{3}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & \\,+10(10y)^{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}+5(10y)^{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4}+1(10y)^{0}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{5} \\\\  & =1(100\\,000{y}^{5})(1)+5(10\\,000{y}^{4})(\\frac{1}{2})+10(1\\,000{y}^{3})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & \\,+10(100{y}^{2})(\\frac{1}{8})+5(10y)(\\frac{1}{16})+1(1)(\\frac{1}{32}) \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+\\frac{50\\,000{y}^{4}}{2}+\\frac{10\\,000{y}^{3}}{4}+\\frac{1\\,000{y}^{2}}{8}+\\frac{50y}{16}+\\frac{1}{32} \\\\  & =100\\,000{y}^{5}+25\\,000{y}^{4}+2\\,500{y}^{3}+125{y}^{2}+\\frac{25y}{8}+\\frac{1}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in ascending powers of $y$, the first three terms are\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{32}+\\frac{25y}{8}+125{y}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8cc0e099-5714-599b-80b4-eb693886b0be" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503765}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503841 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503741 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bac5b62-4ee8-545a-966d-5c12ee806664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x-10y{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503009} {:answer/id #uuid "24ced81d-498a-516a-95d5-fb01dede7a9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x+3y{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503051} {:answer/id #uuid "5e2cc4e3-3a7b-5190-9ba6-ed8c80cfcd59" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x-6y{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503165} {:answer/id #uuid "6331dd90-d13b-5bdb-bc74-b83aa99db890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}x+10y{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503172} {:answer/id #uuid "c137176e-2ac9-58c5-84bd-949dafa76422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{y}^{3}-4{y}^{2}x+6y{x}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503368}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186503051} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b892af4-e657-5e7b-967c-44dc67970e0b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186503583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "c989ddc2-999c-5079-ab1b-dc05c9512624" :question/math-academy-id "q-12884" :question/problem "Find, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $x,$ the first three terms of the binomial expansion of ${(y-x)}^{3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compare our expression with the binomial\n\n$$\n(a+b)^{n}.\n$$\n\nHere we have that $n=3,$ $a=y,$ and $b=-x.$ So, the exponents of the binomial expansion must follow the pattern\n\n$$\n{a}^{3}{b}^{0},\\,{a}^{2}{b}^{1},\\,{a}^{1}{b}^{2},\\,{a}^{0}{b}^{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $3$rd row in Pascal's Triangle is $(1,3,3,1).$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(y-x)}^{3} & =1{a}^{3}+3{a}^{2}b+3a{b}^{2}+1{b}^{3} \\\\  & =1(y)^{3}(-x)^{0}+3(y)^{2}(-x)^{1}+3(y)^{1}(-x)^{2}+1(y)^{0}(-x)^{3} \\\\  & =1({y}^{3})(1)+3({y}^{2})(-x)+3(y)({x}^{2})+1(1)(-{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x+3y{x}^{2}-{x}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in descending powers of $y$ and ascending powers of $x,$ the first three terms are\n\n$$\n{y}^{3}-3{y}^{2}x+3y{x}^{2}.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "febb20a8-7bec-598d-9521-3df7ff50a618"}] :activity/title "Expanding Binomials Using Pascal's Triangle" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186502971}]}
